{% comment %}
=====================================================================
 Version status — one row per target version, rolled up in SQL
 ---------------------------------------------------------------------
 Set the template's OUTPUT to "One document for the whole set" and its
 SOURCE to "Issues".

 `{% version_rollup %}` does in one query what a
 `{% for version %}{% for issue %}` loop does in hundreds: it groups the
 report's issues by target version and returns one row per version, with
 the counts, the hours and the dates already summed.

 `closed_statuses:` names the statuses that count as finished. Without it
 the tag uses Redmine's own `is_closed` flag, which is usually what you
 want — pass names only when your workflow has a status that is closed in
 practice and not in the database.

 Issues with no target version become a row called "None". That row has
 no `version` object, which is why every link below is conditional: a
 report that linked to a version that does not exist would be pointing
 its reader at a 404.
=====================================================================
{% endcomment %}

{% version_rollup from: issues, assign_to: versions %}

<h1>Version status — {{ project.name }}</h1>

<div class="rrd-card">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Version</th>
        <th>Due</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Open</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Closed</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Total</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Estimated</th>
        <th class="rrd-number">Spent</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {% for v in versions %}
        <tr>
          <td>
            {% if v.version %}
              <a href="{{ v.version.url }}">{{ v.name }}</a>
            {% else %}
              {{ v.name }}
            {% endif %}
          </td>
          <td>{{ v.due_date }}</td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ v.open }}</td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ v.closed }}</td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ v.total }}</td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ v.est_hours }}</td>
          <td class="rrd-number">{{ v.spent_hours }}</td>
        </tr>
      {% endfor %}
    </tbody>
  </table>
</div>

{% comment %} The same rollup as a chart, so the shape of the release is visible before the
   numbers are read. `{% chart %}` needs a bucket list, and a rollup row is not one — so the
   breakdown below is a second aggregation over the same issues rather than a reshaping of
   the rows above. Two queries, both in SQL, neither of them a loop. {% endcomment %}
{% sql_aggregate from: issues, group_by: version, drill: true, assign_to: by_version %}

<div class="rrd-card">
  {% chart id: versions, from: by_version, orientation: horizontal,
     title: "Issues by target version" %}
</div>

<p class="rrd-muted">
  Versions with no issues in this report do not appear: the rollup groups the report's own
  issues, so it describes the selection rather than the project.
</p>
